Generics: Generics allow a class, interface, or method to work with different data types while maintaining compile-time type safety.
Why Java introduced Generics?
Before Java 5
Collection<Object>
Everything stored as Object.
Problems
	1. Manual casting 
	2. Runtime Exceptions 
	3. Difficult maintenance 
Generics solved all these.

T → Type
E → Element
K → Key
V → Value
N → Number
R → Return Type
Q1. Why Generics?
	• Type safety 
	• No casting 
	• Reusable code 
	• Compile-time checking 
Q2. Difference between T and ?
	• T is a named type parameter that lets you declare relationships between parameters and return types. 
	• ? is an unknown type used mainly when consuming existing generic types (for example, List<?>). 
Q3. What is PECS?
	• Producer Extends (? extends T) → read values. 
	• Consumer Super (? super T) → write values. 
Q4. What is Type Erasure?
The compiler removes generic type information during compilation. At runtime, List<String> and List<Integer> are both just List.

Summary
	• String extends Object, so a String can be assigned to an Object variable.
	• List<String> does not extend List<Object> because that would allow inserting non-String objects into a list that is supposed to contain only Strings.
	• Java Generics are invariant to guarantee compile-time type safety.
	• Wildcards (?) let you write methods that can accept lists of any element type without sacrificing that safety.
	• List<?> is ideal when you only need to read from a list, not modify it.

